 ## Subtopics
@@ -196,14 +260,14 @@
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method splits processing into two paths. The fast path uses 2D linear/binomial search, which is $O(1)$ per vertex. The slow path involves solving a least-squares system for QEF, which is more expensive but only applied to ambiguous cases [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for occupancy grid, lookup tables, and vertex positions. The QEF step requires temporary storage for matrices $A$ and $b$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** QEF minimization is complex and penalizes performance on parallel hardware due to register limits compared to simpler metrics [S2, unofficial]. QEM is a lighter alternative for volume preservation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces watertight meshes; handles ambiguous cases better than standard linear interpolation; preserves volume [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Higher computational cost for ambiguous cases; register usage can be high on GPUs [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When high-quality surface reconstruction is needed for ambiguous topologies in voxel grids [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When performance is critical and ambiguous cases are rare or can be approximated [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: The method splits processing into two groups. Fast cells use a 2D search, which is $O(1)$ per cell. Slow cells use QEF minimization, which involves solving a least-squares system, typically $O(k^3)$ where $k$ is the number of edges/vertices in the local ring (small constant). Overall, it is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of surface cells.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storage for the occupancy grid, lookup tables, and the output vertex list. The lookup tables are precomputed and constant size relative to the grid. Memory is $O(N)$ for the output.
+*   **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths**: Produces watertight meshes with improved geometric accuracy in ambiguous regions compared to standard Marching Cubes. The hybrid approach balances speed and accuracy.
+*   **Limitations**: More complex to implement than standard Marching Cubes. The QEF solver may be slower for highly ambiguous scenes.
+*   **When to Use**: When high-quality surface reconstruction is needed, especially in regions with topological ambiguities, and performance is not the primary constraint.
+*   **When Not to Use**: When real-time performance is critical and the scene has few ambiguous regions, standard Marching Cubes or QEM-based relocation may be preferable.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the ODC (Octree-based Data Compression) project by KAIST Visual AI Group [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to improve surface extraction quality from voxel data by optimizing vertex positions using QEF [S1, unofficial]. The QEM-based variant was proposed for terrain visualization to reduce computational complexity on parallel hardware [S2, unofficial].
+The method originates from the ODC (Octree-based Dual Contouring) project by KAIST Visual AI Group [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to improve the quality of surface extraction from voxel grids while maintaining the simplicity of the Marching Cubes topology. The QEF optimization is inspired by the work of Hoppe et al. on Quadric Error Metrics [S2, unofficial].
 
